{"y":2005,"label":"2004–05","coach":"Jim Calhoun","conf":"Big East","confShort":"Big East","w":23,"l":8,"cw":13,"cl":3,"confFinish":"2nd in Big East","finish":"r32","seed":2,"region":"Syracuse","apPre":8,"apHigh":7,"apFinal":13,"srs":18.81,"sos":7.62,"ortg":108.5,"drtg":92.9,"ppg":78.0,"oppg":66.8,"story":{"headline":"Calhoun's 700th, a Hall of Fame Year","text":"After losing two top-three draft picks, UConn reloaded around sophomore Charlie Villanueva (13.6 points, 8.3 rebounds), Big East Defensive Player of the Year Josh Boone, Big East Most Improved Player Marcus Williams and Big East Rookie of the Year Rudy Gay. The Huskies went 23–8 and 13–3 to share the Big East regular-season title, and on March 2, 2005, Jim Calhoun earned his 700th career win against Georgetown at Gampel Pavilion. Syracuse edged UConn 67–63 in the Big East semifinals. As a No. 2 seed in Worcester, the Huskies beat Central Florida before NC State upset them 65–62 in the second round. Villanueva left for the NBA as the seventh overall pick — and later that year Calhoun was inducted into the Naismith Memorial Basketball Hall of Fame.","moments":[{"date":"2005-03-02","title":"Calhoun's 700th win","text":"Jim Calhoun earns career win No. 700 against Georgetown at Gampel Pavilion."},{"date":"2005-03-18","title":"NCAA First Round: W vs Central Florida","text":"UConn beat Central Florida 77–71 in the NCAA tournament first round at DCU Center, Worcester, MA."},{"date":"2005-03-20","title":"NCAA Second Round: L vs NC State","text":"UConn lost to NC State 62–65 in the NCAA tournament second round at DCU Center, Worcester, MA."}],"honors":["Big East regular-season co-champions","Josh Boone – Big East Defensive Player of the Year","Rudy Gay – Big East Rookie/Freshman of the Year","Marcus Williams – Big East Most Improved Player"],"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2004%E2%80%9305_Connecticut_Huskies_men's_basketball_team","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UConn_Huskies_men%27s_basketball","https://en